5. CROWD ADMIN AND MEMBER PERMISSIONS

There are two roles.

Crowd Admin

The creator automatically becomes Admin.

Admin can:

- View all active members.
- View all shared member locations.
- Create and share invitation links.
- Revoke or regenerate invitation links.
- Configure geofence centre and radius.
- Configure boundary-alert preferences.
- View crowd activity.
- Participate in chat.
- Delete the entire crowd.

Only the Admin can permanently delete a crowd.

Crowd Members

Every member can:

- View all other active members' shared locations.
- View the live crowd map.
- View member names and avatars.
- Send messages.
- Mention other members.
- Receive boundary alerts.
- Change their own display name.
- Change their own avatar.
- Stop their location sharing.
- Leave the crowd.

Members cannot:

- Delete the crowd.
- Change Admin-only geofence settings.
- Revoke another member's location consent.
- Manage another member's account or permissions.

IMPORTANT: The map is shared equally. The Admin and ordinary members must see the same available crowd locations. Do not implement Admin-only tracking.

6. CRITICAL FEATURE — CONTINUOUS BACKGROUND LOCATION TRACKING

This is one of the most important features in PINPOINT.

Location sharing must continue while the application is in the background, when the screen is locked, and when the user switches to other applications.

Android implementation

Implement a genuine native Android foreground location service.

Requirements:

- Use the appropriate Android foreground service type for location.
- Display a persistent operating-system notification while tracking.
- Use background location permissions only when justified and properly explained.
- Handle Android 10+ background location permission requirements.
- Handle newer Android foreground-service permission and notification requirements.
- Maintain location updates when the user switches applications.
- Maintain tracking when the screen is locked.
- Design the service to survive ordinary removal of the app from the recent-apps screen where supported.
- Reconnect to the backend after temporary network loss.
- Persist the user's active crowd and sharing state securely.
- Restore the tracking service when permitted after normal process recreation or device restart, subject to Android restrictions and renewed permissions.
- Provide a persistent notification with a clear STOP SHARING action.
- Do not use deceptive notification text or hide tracking activity.

Expected Android experience: Swiping PINPOINT away from recent apps must not intentionally stop the location service. Sharing continues until the member manually stops it, leaves the crowd, loses permission, or the operating system/device prevents it.

Do not claim that tracking can survive a user force-stop through Android Settings, permission revocation, device shutdown, or every manufacturer's battery-management restrictions.

Provide instructions for users whose phones require special battery settings.

iOS implementation

Use Apple's permitted background location capabilities and background-location modes.

Continue tracking when supported by iOS and while the user has granted the required permissions.

Clearly disclose that force-quitting an iOS app can stop background location execution. Do not claim that iOS can guarantee uninterrupted tracking after force-quit.

Location-sharing controls

Every member must have an obvious control:

STOP SHARING LOCATION

When pressed:

- Stop sending GPS updates immediately.
- Update the member's status.
- Inform the crowd that the location is no longer updating where appropriate.
- Stop the background tracking service.
- Preserve the member's chat access unless they also leave the crowd.

Also provide:

START SHARING LOCATION

When pressed:

- Explain permissions if necessary.
- Request consent.
- Start the location service.
- Publish updated location.

Do not silently restart tracking after the member manually stops it.

Location states

Display:

- Live.
- Connecting.
- Location unavailable.
- Permission denied.
- Sharing stopped.
- Last updated.
- Last known location.

Never display an old location as a live location.

Use timestamps and stale-location indicators.

