10. CROWD DELETION AND COMPLETE DATA CLEANUP

Only the Crowd Admin can delete a crowd.

When Admin presses DELETE CROWD:

- Show a clear confirmation.
- Explain that all members will lose access.
- Stop all active crowd location sharing.
- Stop or revoke crowd background-tracking sessions.
- Revoke invitation links.
- End the crowd chat.
- Remove crowd memberships.
- Delete crowd-specific location records.
- Delete crowd messages and associated media according to the disclosed retention policy.
- Invalidate relevant access tokens.
- Remove geofence settings and boundary events.
- Notify members that the crowd has ended.

After deletion, the crowd must not remain accessible.

If the same users create a new crowd, it must be a completely fresh crowd with a new ID, new invitation and no previous crowd history.

Implement server-side authorization so ordinary members cannot delete a crowd by changing a URL or API request.

Deletion must be real, not merely hiding the crowd from the interface.

Use a documented backup-deletion cycle and disclose any legally required retained payment/security records separately.

